window.WIKI_SEARCH = [{"id":"custom-content","title":"Custom content","text":"Custom content Know which changes can reuse game assets and which need an asset pipeline. Lua-only mods Lua is useful for changing behaviour, creating UI and assembling objects from assets already cooked into the game. You can prototype layouts by spawning existing meshes and components in the current world. That does not create a new cooked map. The scene still belongs to the existing world, with its lighting, streaming and lifecycle. New models and textures An external .fbx file is a source model, not a game-ready Unreal asset. A Lua script cannot simply use an FBX path as a normal skeletal mesh reference. Proper mesh, material and animation integration generally needs compatible cooked assets or a dedicated runtime importer. This wiki does not provide an importer. Opening the game in Unreal The installed game contains cooked content, rather than the developer’s editable source project. Installing Unreal Editor does not restore the original Blueprints, levels or source assets. The local executable reported the UE5.7 release branch. That is useful context when investigating tools, but it does not establish that any specific editor patch will produce compatible content. Testing a runtime scene Check mesh and material availability in the actual game build. Test lighting and exposure in the world where you spawn objects. Verify collision and a safe player spawn. Keep references to the actors your mod owns. Remove them when the scene closes or the world changes. Further reading Dmgvol’s UE modding guides cover several Unreal modding approaches. Start with Tools & resources for the loader and archive-inspection references used here."},{"id":"debugging","title":"Debugging","text":"Debugging Start with the log and a repeatable set of steps. Read the log In the layout used here, the loader log is Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Check its timestamp and search for your mod’s prefix. Log when your script loads, when a hook installs and when the relevant callback runs. Those are three different checks. local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Script loaded.\") Common errors Symptom What to check No log message Mod folder, enabled.txt, script filename and extraction location. Object is invalid Whether the asset is loaded and whether the world or menu changed. Hook never runs Exact function path, loaded class and whether the game uses that event. TrivialObject is not callable Whether the method is reflected in this game build. Freeze while iterating Container type, loop bounds and recursive hook calls. Native access violation The full crash stack, last callback and recently accessed engine objects. Handle Lua errors A protected call can log a Lua failure and stop that operation: local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Run one operation you are investigating here. end) if not ok then print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end pcall does not make an invalid native pointer safe. If the process crashes inside the engine or loader, simplify the callback and inspect the native stack. Reproduce a problem Write down the game build, loader version and mod version. Use a minimal setup with the mods needed for the test. Record the exact weapon, part or menu involved. Compare the result before and after the operation. Restart fully, then repeat the same steps. For a stat problem, record the value before fitting a part, after fitting it, and after removing it. “It went negative” doesn’t show whether the value dropped below its original baseline. After a game update Check function paths and signatures again. Confirm asset availability, menu construction and save behaviour separately. A successful startup log only proves that startup completed."},{"id":"first-mod","title":"Your first mod","text":"Your first mod Create a mod folder, write one Lua file, and check that it runs. Create the folder Inside your loader’s Mods folder, create MyFirstMod . Add a Scripts folder and an empty file named enabled.txt . ue4ss/Mods/ └── MyFirstMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Enable file extensions in Windows Explorer so the script isn’t accidentally saved as main.lua.txt . Write the script Open main.lua in a text editor and add: local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. message .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Loaded successfully.\") Use a distinct prefix in every log message. It makes your output easy to find when several mods are enabled. Run the mod Save the file. Fully close and restart the game. Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Search for [MyFirstMod] Loaded successfully. If the message appears, your script is loading. If it doesn’t, check the folder names and the log’s Lua errors. Add a keybind This example logs a message when you press F9. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod. RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] F9 pressed.\\n\") end Keep engine work on the game thread A log message is a small first test. If your keybind changes Unreal objects, schedule that work with ExecuteInGameThread . See Finding game objects . Disable or share the mod With the game closed, remove the mod’s enabled.txt to disable it. For a small Lua-only release, package your mod folder, scripts and enable marker with clear installation instructions. Don’t bundle the game, saves, logs or your entire UE4SS installation."},{"id":"hooks","title":"Working with hooks","text":"Working with hooks A hook runs your code when the game calls an Unreal function. Find a function Use the UE4SS object viewer or an object dump to locate the exact reflected function path. Class names and function names are case-sensitive. A Blueprint function often has a path like this: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive This path was observed in the demo’s attachment UI. It may not be loaded until workshop assets are present. Wait for the function Don’t assume a Blueprint is available when main.lua first runs. Check for it on the game thread, and register your hook once. local path = \"/Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive\" local installed = false LoopAsync(2000, function() if installed then return end ExecuteInGameThread(function() if installed then return end local fn = StaticFindObject(path) if not fn or not fn:IsValid() then return end RegisterHook(path, function(context) local object = context:get() if not object or not object:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] Workshop lookup called.\\n\") end) installed = true print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hook installed.\\n\") end) end Read parameters carefully Hook arguments can be UE4SS parameter wrappers. Use the access pattern documented for the parameter’s type, and inspect the signature before reading or changing it. Callback phase and return-parameter behaviour can differ between native and Blueprint functions. The sample above only reads the context and logs a call. It does not alter mount compatibility or attachments. Avoid common mistakes Don’t install the same hook on every timer tick. Don’t call the hooked function from its own callback unless you have a deliberate recursion guard. Don’t assume a path from an older game build is still correct. Don’t use a Lua error handler as protection against native memory crashes. Check the UE4SS API documentation for your loader version before writing to parameters or relying on pre/post callbacks."},{"id":"installation","title":"Installing UE4SS","text":"Installing UE4SS Set up the loader, then check the log before adding a mod. What you need The Windows version of Ballistic.exe. A UE4SS release compatible with your game build. A text editor, such as Visual Studio Code. The local demo tests used UE4SS 3.0.1 Beta #0 , commit 5e627997 . This records a tested setup; it is not a recommendation to overwrite a working installation with a different release. Downloads: UE4SS releases · Experimental builds . Find the game folder In Steam, open the game’s settings and choose Manage → Browse local files . From there, open: Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ You should see Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe . The smaller Ballistic.exe in the game’s top-level folder is a launcher. Install the loader beside the shipping executable. Install the loader Close the game. Extract the chosen UE4SS package beside the shipping executable, preserving the package’s folder layout. Merge folders carefully if you already have other mods installed. Launch the game once, then close it and inspect the log. The setup used for these guides has the following layout. Other loader packages can use a different working directory; follow the installation instructions for your release . Win64/ ├── Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe ├── dwmapi.dll └── ue4ss/ ├── UE4SS.dll ├── UE4SS-settings.ini ├── UE4SS.log └── Mods/ Check the log Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Look for the loader version and startup messages. The file should have a fresh timestamp from your last launch. If it is missing or unchanged, check the extraction location and that the files match your loader release. If the game crashes before mods load, resolve the loader setup before adding scripts. Next step Continue to Your first mod to create a script and verify it with a unique log message."},{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Introduction A practical guide to modding Ballistic.exe with UE4SS and Lua. This wiki covers setting up your mod folder, finding game objects, hooking functions and troubleshooting scripts. Start with a small change, check it in-game, and build from there. You don’t need Unreal Editor for the Lua examples here. UE4SS loads your scripts alongside the game and lets them interact with objects and functions that Unreal exposes at runtime. Before you start The examples come from the Windows demo. Game updates can change class names, functions and file layouts. Keep a backup of your saves and check your own build’s log when something behaves differently. Getting started If you’re new to Unreal modding, follow these pages in order. Guide What you’ll do Installing UE4SS Set up the loader and check that it starts. Your first mod Create a Lua script and find its output in the log. Working with hooks Run code when a game function is called. Debugging Track down missing objects, Lua errors and crashes. What can you do with Lua? Lua mods can inspect loaded objects, call reflected functions, respond to game events and create UI using assets the game already contains. Some changes need only a few lines of code. Adjust workshop behaviour and add controls to existing menus. Read weapon or target information for tools and overlays. Spawn and arrange existing assets at runtime. Add keybinds, diagnostics and small quality-of-life changes. These are starting points, not a guarantee that every game system is accessible. See Custom content for the limits of a Lua-only mod. A small example A mod begins with Scripts/main.lua . This is enough to check that the loader is running your file: print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hello from Ballistic!\\n\") The message appears in ue4ss/UE4SS.log after you launch the game. The first mod guide shows the complete folder structure. Using this wiki Use the sidebar to browse, or press Ctrl + K to search the text of every guide. Each article has links to its sections and a copy button beside code examples. For the complete loader API, use the UE4SS documentation . The game-specific paths here are observations from the demo, rather than a supported game SDK."},{"id":"objects","title":"Finding game objects","text":"Finding game objects Find an object, check that it is valid, then use it while it still belongs to the current world. Loaded objects StaticFindObject looks up an object that is already loaded. It does not automatically load every asset from the game archive. ExecuteInGameThread(function() local object = StaticFindObject( \"/Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data\" ) if not object or not object:IsValid() then print(\"[MyFirstMod] Attachment table is not loaded yet.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Instances and defaults A class describes an object type. A class default object contains its default values. A live actor or widget is an instance. Editing a default is not the same as changing the weapon or menu the player is currently using. Read the full object name and confirm which object you found before writing to it. World changes Travelling from the bunker to a range replaces many actors and widgets. A reference that worked before travel can become invalid afterwards. Reacquire world-specific objects after travel. Check validity immediately before use. Clear cached widget references when the menu is destroyed. Keep track of actors your mod spawns so you can clean them up. Arrays and containers Unreal containers exposed through UE4SS are not always ordinary Lua tables. In this local loader, using ipairs on some Unreal arrays continued beyond the reported length. A bounded loop worked for those arrays: local points = editor.POIs local count = #points assert(count \u003c= 512, \"Unexpected attachment-point count\") for i = 1, count do local point = points[i] if point and point:IsValid() then print(point:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end This example requires a valid editor instance. Set and map containers have different APIs; don’t apply the array pattern to every container. Reflected methods Use methods present in the dump for your build. For actor transforms, the local bindings exposed K2_GetActorLocation and K2_GetActorRotation . Calling an unavailable method produced a TrivialObject error rather than a normal Lua function call."},{"id":"paths","title":"Game paths & classes","text":"Game paths & classes Useful locations and names observed in the Windows demo. Build-specific reference These paths were checked locally on Steam demo build 26083142 . They are a starting point for discovery, not a promise that another release has the same objects. File locations Location Purpose Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ Shipping executable and loader entry point. Win64/ue4ss/Mods/ Mod folders in the tested loader layout. Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log Loader and Lua output; paths here start at Binaries. %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/ Player saves. Keep these out of mod releases. Ballistic/Content/Paks/ Cooked game content archives. Workshop functions The attachment point widget uses this class path: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C Function Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup. OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup creation. SetupAttachment Fitting or removing an attachment. The workshop HUD is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is named WeaponEditMOde ; preserve that spelling when looking it up. Weapon classes /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart The Blueprint weapon base exposes UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . In the inspected build it has nine boolean inputs. Check the full signature in your own dump before calling it. Maps Name Observed use Bunker_Main Bunker and workshop. Range_SwimmingPool Azure Swimming Pool range. The confirmed pool package is /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool . A range row or menu label alone does not prove that its associated map is included in the demo. Check your build Use the loader’s object viewer or dump to confirm paths. Keep a small text record of the build, object name, signature and action you tested. See Debugging for a repeatable check."},{"id":"tools","title":"Tools & resources","text":"Tools & resources The main references to keep open while you work. UE4SS The loader runs Lua mods and exposes Unreal objects and functions. Match API guidance to your installed version; the development documentation may describe APIs newer than your local loader. Releases and downloads Installation guide Development documentation Lua API overview Game inspection Start with the UE4SS object viewer and object dump to inspect what the current game session has loaded. Use an archive browser when you need to look for cooked content that isn’t loaded yet. FModel — Unreal archive browser. FModel getting started — configuration and usage. Tool support depends on the game’s engine and archive format. Finding an asset in an archive does not guarantee it can be loaded in every game mode. Unreal modding UE_Modding by Dmgvol collects techniques and guides for Unreal games. Use it to research asset-based mods alongside the Lua approach described here. The game Ballistic.exe on Steam This wiki documents local observations and practical examples. It does not ship a game framework, SDK or game assets."}];
